OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Symplectic-Balls-in-Symmetric-Polar-Products-September-22-2026", "title": "Symplectic Balls in Symmetric Polar Products", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Symplectic-Balls-in-Symmetric-Polar-Products-September-22-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "087", "family_title": "The Mahler conjectures, functional inequalities and polar-product symplectic width", "abstract_html": "For every integer <i>n</i> ≥ 2 and every origin-symmetric convex body \\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\), we prove that the Gromov width of \\(\\mathop{\\mathrm{int}}\\nolimits K\\times\\mathop{\\mathrm{int}}\\nolimits K^\\circ\\) is 4. We construct smooth symplectic embeddings of standard balls of every capacity \\(0\\lt c\\lt 4\\) into this polar product. Volume preservation then resolves the symmetric Mahler conjecture positively in every dimension.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( c_G(U_K)=4. \\) More precisely, for every \\(0<c<4\\), there is a smooth symplectic embedding \\( B^{2n}(c)\\hookrightarrow U_K. \\) No smoothness, strict convexity, unconditional symmetry, or product structure is assumed …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\geq 2\\) be an integer.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\) be an origin-symmetric convex body: \\(K\\) is compact, convex, has nonempty interior, and satisfies \\(K=-K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define its polar by\n  \\[\n  K^\\circ=\\{p\\in\\mathbb R^n:\\langle q,p\\rangle\\leq 1\n  \\text{ for every }q\\in K\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Equip \\(\\mathbb R_q^n\\times\\mathbb R_p^n\\) with\n  \\[\n  \\omega_0=\\sum_{j=1}^n dq_j\\wedge dp_j,\n  \\qquad\n  U_K=\\operatorname{int}K\\times\\operatorname{int}K^\\circ.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(c&gt;0\\), write\n  \\[\n  B^{2n}(c)=\\{(q,p):\\pi(|q|^2+|p|^2)&lt;c\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The Gromov width \\(c_G(U)\\) of an open set \\(U\\subset\\mathbb R^{2n}\\) is the supremum of those \\(c&gt;0\\) for which there is a smooth embedding \\(e:B^{2n}(c)\\to U\\) satisfying \\(e^*\\omega_0=\\omega_0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nc_G(U_K)=4.\n\\]\nMore precisely, for every \\(0&lt;c&lt;4\\), there is a smooth symplectic embedding\n\\[\nB^{2n}(c)\\hookrightarrow U_K.\n\\]\nNo smoothness, strict convexity, unconditional symmetry, or product structure is assumed for \\(K\\), and no embedding at capacity \\(4\\) is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(0&lt;c&lt;4\\). Choose \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\lambda c&lt;4,\\qquad \\lambda=1+\\alpha,\n\\]\nand choose one finite strip body \\(K'\\) from Proposition 5. Proposition 4 gives a smooth symplectic embedding\n\\[\ne:B^{2n}(\\lambda c)\\longrightarrow\n\\operatorname{int}K'\\times\\operatorname{int}((K')^\\circ).\n\\]</p><p>If one body is contained in another, every interior ball in the first is contained in the second. Thus\n\\[\nK\\subset K'\\subset\\lambda K,\n\\qquad\n(K')^\\circ\\subset K^\\circ\n\\]\nimplies\n\\[\n\\operatorname{int}K'\\subset\\lambda\\operatorname{int}K,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{int}((K')^\\circ)\\subset\\operatorname{int}K^\\circ,\n\\]\neven when their boundaries meet. Regard \\(e\\) as an embedding into\n\\[\n\\lambda\\operatorname{int}K\\times\\operatorname{int}K^\\circ.\n\\]</p><p>Define\n\\[\nD_{\\sqrt\\lambda}(z)=\\sqrt\\lambda\\,z,\n\\qquad\nQ_\\lambda(q,p)=(q/\\lambda,p).\n\\]\nThe composition\n\\[\nQ_\\lambda\\circ e\\circ D_{\\sqrt\\lambda}\n:\nB^{2n}(c)\\longrightarrow U_K\n\\]\nis a smooth embedding. Since\n\\[\nD_{\\sqrt\\lambda}^*\\omega_0=\\lambda\\omega_0,\n\\qquad\nQ_\\lambda^*\\omega_0=\\lambda^{-1}\\omega_0,\n\\]\nits pullback of \\(\\omega_0\\) equals \\(\\omega_0\\). This proves\n\\[\nc_G(U_K)\\geq4.\n\\]\nProposition 6 gives \\(c_G(U_K)\\leq4\\), and therefore\n\\[\nc_G(U_K)=4.\n\\]</p><p>The order of choices is that of the paper’s Remark 5.2. For each prescribed \\(c&lt;4\\), first choose \\(\\alpha\\), then fix \\(K'\\) and its \\(m\\) defining constraints. Choose \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\n(1+\\eta)\\lambda c&lt;4,\n\\]\nand then choose \\(k\\) large enough that\n\\[\n\\frac{C_{K'}}k+m\\epsilon_k&lt;\\frac{\\eta\\pi}{4}.\n\\]\nThis is possible because the constraint list is fixed before \\(k\\) varies. Proposition 4, applied at capacity \\(\\lambda c\\), and the final rescaling give the required ball. Each prescribed capacity is obtained by one finite construction; no limit of embeddings is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary 5.3; sharpness is recorded in Remark 5.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\operatorname{vol}_n(K)\\operatorname{vol}_n(K^\\circ) \\geq \\frac{4^n}{n!}. \\) The constant is sharp.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\geq 1\\) be an integer.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\) be an origin-symmetric convex body.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  K^\\circ=\\{p\\in\\mathbb R^n:\\langle q,p\\rangle\\leq 1\n  \\text{ for every }q\\in K\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\operatorname{vol}_d\\) for \\(d\\)-dimensional Lebesgue measure.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\operatorname{vol}_n(K)\\operatorname{vol}_n(K^\\circ)\n\\geq \\frac{4^n}{n!}.\n\\]\nThe constant is sharp.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(n=1\\), write \\(K=[-a,a]\\) with \\(a&gt;0\\). Then\n\\[\nK^\\circ=[-a^{-1},a^{-1}],\n\\]\nand the product of their lengths is \\(4\\). Assume henceforth that \\(n\\geq2\\).</p><p>First, taking interiors does not change the relevant volumes. If a convex body \\(C\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\) contains \\(B(0,r)\\), then convexity gives, for \\(0&lt;t&lt;1\\),\n\\[\ntC+B(0,(1-t)r)\\subset C.\n\\]\nHence \\(tC\\subset\\operatorname{int}C\\), and\n\\[\nt^n\\operatorname{vol}_n(C)\n\\leq\n\\operatorname{vol}_n(\\operatorname{int}C)\n\\leq\n\\operatorname{vol}_n(C).\n\\]\nLetting \\(t\\uparrow1\\) proves\n\\[\n\\operatorname{vol}_n(\\operatorname{int}C)\n=\n\\operatorname{vol}_n(C).\n\\]\nApply this to \\(K\\) and \\(K^\\circ\\), both of which contain a neighborhood of the origin. The product formula for Lebesgue measure gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{vol}_{2n}(U_K)\n=\n\\operatorname{vol}_n(K)\\operatorname{vol}_n(K^\\circ).\n\\]</p><p>For every \\(0&lt;c&lt;4\\), Theorem 1 supplies a smooth symplectic embedding\n\\[\nf:B^{2n}(c)\\longrightarrow U_K.\n\\]\nTaking the \\(n\\)-th exterior power of \\(f^*\\omega_0=\\omega_0\\) shows that \\(\\det Df=1\\), so the change-of-variables formula gives\n\\[\n\\frac{c^n}{n!}\n=\n\\operatorname{vol}_{2n}(B^{2n}(c))\n=\n\\operatorname{vol}_{2n}(f(B^{2n}(c)))\n\\leq\n\\operatorname{vol}_{2n}(U_K).\n\\]\nThe ball-volume equality follows from the Euclidean ball volume\n\\[\n\\frac{\\pi^nR^{2n}}{n!}\n\\]\nat radius \\(R=\\sqrt{c/\\pi}\\). Letting \\(c\\uparrow4\\) proves\n\\[\n\\operatorname{vol}_n(K)\\operatorname{vol}_n(K^\\circ)\n\\geq\\frac{4^n}{n!}.\n\\]</p><p>For sharpness, as in the paper’s Remark 5.4, the cube \\([-1,1]^n\\) has volume \\(2^n\\). Its polar is\n\\[\n\\left\\{p:\\sum_{j=1}^n|p_j|\\leq1\\right\\},\n\\]\nwhose intersection with each coordinate orthant is a simplex of volume \\(1/n!\\). These \\(2^n\\) simplices meet only along faces, so the polar has volume \\(2^n/n!\\). Their volume product is therefore \\(4^n/n!\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Corollary 5.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are symplectic embeddings of open neighborhoods of the closed balls \\( \\overline{B^{2n}(R_1)},\\ldots,\\overline{B^{2n}(R_k)} \\) into \\(U_K\\), with pairwise disjoint images.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\geq 3\\) and \\(k\\geq 1\\) be integers.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\) be an origin-symmetric convex body, with polar \\(K^\\circ\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  U_K=\\operatorname{int}K\\times\\operatorname{int}K^\\circ,\n  \\qquad\n  \\omega_0=\\sum_{j=1}^n dq_j\\wedge dp_j.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(R&gt;0\\), let\n  \\[\n  B^{2n}(R)=\\{(q,p):\\pi(|q|^2+|p|^2)&lt;R\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(R_1,\\ldots,R_k&gt;0\\) satisfy\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{i=1}^k R_i^n&lt;4^n,\n  \\qquad\n  R_i+R_j&lt;4\\quad(1\\leq i&lt;j\\leq k).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are symplectic embeddings of open neighborhoods of the closed balls\n\\[\n\\overline{B^{2n}(R_1)},\\ldots,\\overline{B^{2n}(R_k)}\n\\]\ninto \\(U_K\\), with pairwise disjoint images. The pairwise condition is vacuous when \\(k=1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Strictness and finiteness permit a choice of \\(0&lt;c&lt;4\\) such that\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^kR_i^n&lt;c^n,\n\\qquad\nR_i+R_j&lt;c\\quad\\text{for every }i&lt;j.\n\\]\nThe ball-packing theorem cited in the paper as [16, Theorem 1.1], from “Symplectic Ball Packings in Higher Dimensions,” embeds these closed balls into \\(B^{2n}(c)\\), with embeddings defined on neighborhoods.</p><p>Their compact images are pairwise disjoint and lie in the open target, so the neighborhoods can be shrunk to have pairwise disjoint images contained in \\(B^{2n}(c)\\). Composing with the embedding\n\\[\nB^{2n}(c)\\hookrightarrow U_K\n\\]\nfrom Theorem 1 proves the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Theorem 2.1, used as an intermediate result in the proof of Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(0<c<\\pi k\\), there is a smooth symplectic embedding \\( (B^{2n}(c),\\omega_0)\\longrightarrow(V,\\omega). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n,m\\geq 1\\), let \\(\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb C^n\\) be open and contain \\(0\\), and let \\(f=(f_1,\\ldots,f_m):\\Omega\\to\\mathbb C^m\\) be holomorphic.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that, for an integer \\(k\\geq 1\\),\n  \\[\n  f^{-1}(0)=\\{0\\},\n  \\qquad\n  |f(z)|=O(|z|^k)\\quad\\text{as }z\\to 0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that, for every \\(0&lt;s&lt;1\\),\n  \\[\n  \\{z\\in\\Omega:|f(z)|^2\\leq s\\}\n  \\]\n  is a compact subset of \\(\\Omega\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(z=u+ix\\), and use\n  \\[\n  d^c\\phi(X)=-\\frac14\\,d\\phi(iX),\n  \\qquad\n  dd^c\\phi=d(d^c\\phi).\n  \\]\n  Thus\n  \\[\n  \\lambda_0=d^c|z|^2\n  =\\frac12\\sum_{j=1}^n(u_j\\,dx_j-x_j\\,du_j),\n  \\qquad\n  \\omega_0=dd^c|z|^2\n  =\\sum_{j=1}^n du_j\\wedge dx_j.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  \\tau=|f|^2,\\qquad\n  V=\\{z\\in\\Omega:\\tau(z)&lt;1\\},\n  \\qquad\n  \\omega=dd^c(|z|^2+\\tau).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">In these coordinates,\n  \\[\n  B^{2n}(c)=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C^n:\\pi|z|^2&lt;c\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(0&lt;c&lt;\\pi k\\), there is a smooth symplectic embedding\n\\[\n(B^{2n}(c),\\omega_0)\\longrightarrow(V,\\omega).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(0&lt;a&lt;k\\); we will embed the ball of capacity \\(\\pi a\\). Choose\n\\[\n\\frac ak&lt;b&lt;1,\\qquad a&lt;\\mu&lt;bk.\n\\]\nLemma 1.1 supplies the logarithmic potential \\(H\\). Lemma 1.2 replaces its singularity by a smooth plurisubharmonic function \\(\\widetilde H\\), equal on \\(B_{r_1}\\) to\n\\[\n\\mu\\log(|z|^2+\\delta)-M,\n\\]\nwith \\(\\widetilde H-\\tau\\) compactly supported in \\(V\\). Lemma 1.3 supplies the symplectic transfer\n\\[\n\\varphi_1^{-1}:(V,\\widetilde\\omega)\\longrightarrow(V,\\omega),\n\\qquad\n\\widetilde\\omega=dd^c(|z|^2+\\widetilde H).\n\\]</p><p>On \\(B_{r_1}\\), the new potential, up to the additive constant \\(-M\\), is \\(\\Psi(|z|^2)\\), where\n\\[\n\\Psi(t)=t+\\mu\\log(t+\\delta).\n\\]\nDefine the radial map\n\\[\nR(z)=\\sqrt{\\Psi'(|z|^2)}\\,z\n=\\sqrt{1+\\frac{\\mu}{|z|^2+\\delta}}\\,z.\n\\]\nIn the pullback of\n\\[\n\\lambda_0=\\frac12\\sum_{j=1}^n(u_j\\,dx_j-x_j\\,du_j),\n\\]\nthe terms differentiating the radial factor cancel. Hence\n\\[\nR^*\\lambda_0\n=\n\\Psi'(|z|^2)\\lambda_0\n=\nd^c\\Psi(|z|^2),\n\\qquad\nR^*\\omega_0=\\widetilde\\omega|_{B_{r_1}}.\n\\]\nThis is the radial symplectic-coordinate calculation cited in the paper in [13, proof of Theorem 1.1, equation (23)].</p><p>The squared radial coordinate is strictly increasing, because\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(t\\Psi'(t)\\bigr)\n=\n\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t+\\frac{\\mu t}{t+\\delta}\\right)\n=\n1+\\frac{\\mu\\delta}{(t+\\delta)^2}&gt;0.\n\\]\nAt zero, the map is smooth and has derivative\n\\[\nDR(0)=\\sqrt{1+\\mu/\\delta}\\,I,\n\\]\nso its inverse is smooth there as well. Thus \\(R\\) is a diffeomorphism from \\(B_{r_1}\\) onto\n\\[\nB^{2n}(\\pi Q_\\delta),\n\\qquad\nQ_\\delta=r_1^2+\\frac{\\mu r_1^2}{r_1^2+\\delta}&gt;a,\n\\]\nwhere the last inequality follows from\n\\[\n\\delta&lt;r_1^2(\\mu/a-1).\n\\]</p><p>If \\(\\iota:B_{r_1}\\hookrightarrow V\\) denotes inclusion, then\n\\[\n\\varphi_1^{-1}\\circ\\iota\\circ\nR^{-1}|_{B^{2n}(\\pi a)}\n:\nB^{2n}(\\pi a)\\longrightarrow V\n\\]\nis a smooth embedding. Its pullback of \\(\\omega\\) is \\(\\omega_0\\) by the transfer identity and the radial pullback identity. Since \\(a\\) was arbitrary in \\((0,k)\\), the proposition follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The functions \\(\\tau\\) and \\(\\log\\tau\\), the latter on \\(\\Omega\\setminus\\{0\\}\\), are plurisubharmonic.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting and differential-form convention of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0&lt;a&lt;k\\), and choose\n  \\[\n  \\frac ak&lt;b&lt;1,\n  \\qquad\n  a&lt;\\mu&lt;bk.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The functions \\(\\tau\\) and \\(\\log\\tau\\), the latter on \\(\\Omega\\setminus\\{0\\}\\), are plurisubharmonic. There is a smooth plurisubharmonic function \\(H\\) on \\(V\\setminus\\{0\\}\\), a number \\(s_0\\in(0,1)\\), and a constant \\(C_1\\) such that\n\\[\nH=\\tau\\quad\\text{wherever }\\tau\\geq s_0,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nH(z)\\leq bk\\log|z|^2+C_1\n\\]\nfor every sufficiently small \\(z\\neq 0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For every real tangent vector \\(X\\),\n\\[\ndd^c\\phi(X,iX)\n=\\frac14\\Delta_\\zeta\\phi(z+\\zeta X)\\big|_{\\zeta=0},\n\\qquad \\zeta\\in\\mathbb C.\n\\]\nThus a smooth function \\(\\phi\\) is plurisubharmonic precisely when these quantities are nonnegative. In that case \\(dd^c(|z|^2+\\phi)\\) is symplectic: it is closed, and its value on \\((X,iX)\\) is at least \\(|X|^2\\).</p><p>A smooth convex, coordinatewise nondecreasing function of plurisubharmonic functions is plurisubharmonic. Indeed, on each complex line,\n\\[\n\\Delta\\Gamma(h_1,\\ldots,h_N)\n=\n\\sum_{j=1}^N\\Gamma_j\\Delta h_j\n+\n\\sum_{i,j=1}^N\\Gamma_{ij}\n\\langle\\nabla h_i,\\nabla h_j\\rangle\n\\geq 0.\n\\]\nThe first sum is nonnegative by monotonicity, and the second is nonnegative because the Hessian of \\(\\Gamma\\) is positive semidefinite.</p><p>For the holomorphic tuple \\(f\\), differentiation along a complex line gives\n\\[\ndd^c\\tau(X,iX)=|df(X)|^2,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\ndd^c\\log\\tau(X,iX)\n=\n\\frac{\n|f|^2|df(X)|^2\n-\n\\left|\\sum_{j=1}^m\\overline{f_j}\\,df_j(X)\\right|^2\n}{|f|^4}\n\\quad\\text{on }\\Omega\\setminus\\{0\\}.\n\\]\nThe second expression is nonnegative by Cauchy–Schwarz. Hence \\(\\tau\\) and, away from its zero, \\(\\log\\tau\\) are plurisubharmonic.</p><p>Choose\n\\[\n\\log b&lt;t_-&lt;t_+&lt;0\n\\]\nand a smooth nondecreasing cutoff \\(\\beta:\\mathbb R\\to[0,1]\\) which is \\(0\\) for \\(t\\leq t_-\\) and \\(1\\) for \\(t\\geq t_+\\). Define \\(\\chi\\) on \\((-\\infty,0)\\) by\n\\[\n\\chi'(t)=b+\\beta(t)(e^t-b),\n\\]\nchoosing its additive constant so that \\(\\chi(t)=e^t\\) for \\(t\\geq t_+\\). This is possible since \\(\\chi'=e^t\\) there. Moreover, \\(\\chi'&gt;0\\), and\n\\[\n\\chi''(t)=\\beta'(t)(e^t-b)+\\beta(t)e^t\\geq 0,\n\\]\nbecause the transition occurs where \\(e^t&gt;b\\). For \\(t\\leq t_-\\), the function \\(\\chi\\) is affine with slope \\(b\\).</p><p>It follows that\n\\[\nH=\\chi(\\log\\tau)\n\\]\nis smooth and plurisubharmonic on \\(V\\setminus\\{0\\}\\), and\n\\[\nH=\\tau\\quad\\text{wherever }\\tau\\geq s_0:=e^{t_+}.\n\\]\nThe vanishing assumption gives\n\\[\n\\tau(z)\\leq C_0|z|^{2k}\n\\]\nnear zero. Since \\(\\chi\\) is eventually affine, there is a constant \\(C_1\\) such that\n\\[\nH(z)\\leq bk\\log|z|^2+C_1\n\\]\nfor every sufficiently small \\(z\\neq 0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are \\(0<r_1<r_2\\), with \\(\\overline{B_{r_2}}\\subset V\\), a constant \\(M\\), a number \\( 0<\\delta< \\min\\left\\{1,r_1^2\\left(\\frac{\\mu}{a}-1\\right)\\right\\}, \\) and a smooth plurisubharmonic function \\(\\widetilde H\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix\n  \\[\n  0&lt;a&lt;k,\\qquad \\frac ak&lt;b&lt;1,\\qquad a&lt;\\mu&lt;bk.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\) be a smooth plurisubharmonic function on \\(V\\setminus\\{0\\}\\) such that, for some \\(s_0\\in(0,1)\\) and \\(C_1\\in\\mathbb R\\),\n  \\[\n  H=\\tau\\quad\\text{where }\\tau\\geq s_0,\n  \\qquad\n  H(z)\\leq bk\\log|z|^2+C_1\n  \\]\n  for sufficiently small \\(z\\neq 0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(B_r=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C^n:|z|&lt;r\\}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(0&lt;r_1&lt;r_2\\), with \\(\\overline{B_{r_2}}\\subset V\\), a constant \\(M\\), a number\n\\[\n0&lt;\\delta&lt;\n\\min\\left\\{1,r_1^2\\left(\\frac{\\mu}{a}-1\\right)\\right\\},\n\\]\nand a smooth plurisubharmonic function \\(\\widetilde H\\) on \\(V\\) such that\n\\[\n\\widetilde H(z)=\\mu\\log(|z|^2+\\delta)-M\n\\quad\\text{on }B_{r_1},\n\\]\nand \\(\\widetilde H-\\tau\\) has compact support in \\(V\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(r_2&gt;0\\) with \\(\\overline{B_{r_2}}\\subset V\\). For \\(0&lt;\\delta\\leq 1\\), consider\n\\[\nP_\\delta(z)=\\mu\\log(|z|^2+\\delta)-M.\n\\]\nThe functions \\(P_\\delta\\) are plurisubharmonic by the logarithmic calculation in Lemma 1.1, applied to the nowhere-zero holomorphic tuple\n\\[\n(\\sqrt\\delta,z_1,\\ldots,z_n).\n\\]</p><p>Choose \\(M\\) so that\n\\[\nM&gt;\n\\mu\\log(r_2^2+1)-\\min_{|z|=r_2}H(z)+2.\n\\]\nThe minimum is finite because \\(f\\) has no zero on this sphere. Then\n\\[\nP_\\delta&lt;H-2\\quad\\text{on }|z|=r_2\n\\]\nfor every \\(0&lt;\\delta\\leq 1\\). On the other hand,\n\\[\nP_\\delta(z)-H(z)\n\\geq\n(\\mu-bk)\\log|z|^2-M-C_1.\n\\]\nAs \\(z\\to 0\\), this lower bound tends to \\(+\\infty\\), uniformly in \\(\\delta\\). We may therefore fix \\(0&lt;r_1&lt;r_2\\), independently of \\(\\delta\\), such that\n\\[\nP_\\delta&gt;H+2\n\\quad\\text{on }0&lt;|z|\\leq r_1\n\\quad\\text{for every }0&lt;\\delta\\leq 1.\n\\]\nFix one positive \\(\\delta\\) satisfying\n\\[\n0&lt;\\delta&lt;\n\\min\\left\\{1,r_1^2\\left(\\frac{\\mu}{a}-1\\right)\\right\\}.\n\\]\nThe upper bound is positive because \\(\\mu&gt;a\\).</p><p>Replace the singularity of \\(H\\) by \\(P_\\delta\\) using a regularized maximum. Let\n\\[\n\\vartheta\\in C_c^\\infty((-1,1))\n\\]\nbe nonnegative and even, with \\(\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\vartheta=1\\), and set\n\\[\n\\rho(s)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}|s-t|\\vartheta(t)\\,dt,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{rmax}(h,p)\n=\\frac{h+p+\\rho(h-p)}2.\n\\]\nThe function \\(\\rho\\) is smooth and convex, satisfies \\(|\\rho'|\\leq 1\\), and equals \\(|s|\\) when \\(|s|\\geq 1\\). Thus \\(\\operatorname{rmax}\\) is smooth, convex, nondecreasing in both arguments, and equals \\(\\max(h,p)\\) when \\(|h-p|\\geq 1\\).</p><p>Define \\(\\widetilde H\\) to be \\(\\operatorname{rmax}(H,P_\\delta)\\) on \\(B_{r_2}\\setminus\\{0\\}\\), to be \\(H\\) outside \\(B_{r_2}\\), and to take the value \\(P_\\delta(0)\\) at zero. The outer comparison gives equality with \\(H\\) in a neighborhood of \\(\\partial B_{r_2}\\), and the inner comparison gives equality with \\(P_\\delta\\) on \\(B_{r_1}\\). Consequently \\(\\widetilde H\\) is smooth and plurisubharmonic on all of \\(V\\).</p><p>The difference \\(\\widetilde H-\\tau\\) has compact support in \\(V\\). Indeed, set\n\\[\ns_2=\\max_{\\overline{B_{r_2}}}\\tau&lt;1,\n\\]\nchoose\n\\[\n\\max(s_0,s_2)&lt;\\sigma&lt;1,\n\\]\nand put\n\\[\nC=\\{z\\in\\Omega:\\tau(z)\\leq\\sigma\\}.\n\\]\nThis is a compact subset of \\(V\\) by hypothesis. Outside \\(C\\), a point lies outside \\(\\overline{B_{r_2}}\\) and has \\(H=\\tau\\), so \\(\\widetilde H-\\tau\\) vanishes there.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a diffeomorphism \\(\\varphi_1:V\\to V\\) such that \\( \\varphi_1^*\\widetilde\\omega=\\omega. \\) Consequently \\( \\varphi_1^{-1}:(V,\\widetilde\\omega)\\longrightarrow(V,\\omega) \\) is symplectic.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\widetilde H\\) be smooth and plurisubharmonic on \\(V\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that \\(\\widetilde H-\\tau\\) is supported in a compact subset \\(C\\subset V\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\widetilde\\omega=dd^c(|z|^2+\\widetilde H).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a diffeomorphism \\(\\varphi_1:V\\to V\\) such that\n\\[\n\\varphi_1^*\\widetilde\\omega=\\omega.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n\\varphi_1^{-1}:(V,\\widetilde\\omega)\\longrightarrow(V,\\omega)\n\\]\nis symplectic.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Moser’s deformation argument, as cited in the paper in [15, Section 4]. Consider\n\\[\n\\omega_s\n=\ndd^c\\bigl(|z|^2+(1-s)\\tau+s\\widetilde H\\bigr),\n\\qquad 0\\leq s\\leq 1.\n\\]\nEach form is symplectic because the two non-Euclidean potentials are plurisubharmonic. Define the smooth time-dependent vector field \\(Y_s\\) by\n\\[\n\\iota_{Y_s}\\omega_s=-\\gamma,\n\\qquad\n\\gamma=d^c(\\widetilde H-\\tau).\n\\]</p><p>Both \\(\\gamma\\) and every \\(Y_s\\) are supported in the fixed compact set \\(C\\). Extending \\(Y_s\\) by zero outside \\(V\\) gives a smooth vector field on \\(\\mathbb C^n\\), with its coefficients and first derivatives bounded uniformly for \\(s\\in[0,1]\\). The ordinary differential equation for this field has a flow \\(\\varphi_s\\) throughout this time interval. The ambient flow fixes \\(\\mathbb C^n\\setminus V\\) pointwise and hence restricts to diffeomorphisms of \\(V\\). This argument applies even if \\(V\\) is disconnected and places no regularity assumption on its boundary.</p><p>Since \\(d\\omega_s=0\\), differentiation of pullbacks gives\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{ds}\\varphi_s^*\\omega_s\n=\n\\varphi_s^*(d\\gamma+d\\iota_{Y_s}\\omega_s)\n=0.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\varphi_1^*\\widetilde\\omega=\\omega,\n\\]\nand \\(\\varphi_1^{-1}:(V,\\widetilde\\omega)\\to(V,\\omega)\\) is symplectic.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Lemma 3.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The function \\(F\\) is a biholomorphism from \\(\\mathbb D\\) onto a bounded domain \\(D\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\mathbb D=\\{w\\in\\mathbb C:|w|&lt;1\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  F(w)=\\frac8{\\pi^2}\n  \\sum_{\\ell=0}^\\infty\n  \\frac{(-1)^\\ell w^{2\\ell+1}}{(2\\ell+1)^2},\n  \\qquad w\\in\\mathbb D.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D=F(\\mathbb D)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The function \\(F\\) is a biholomorphism from \\(\\mathbb D\\) onto a bounded domain \\(D\\). It is odd, commutes with conjugation, and satisfies \\(F(0)=0\\).</p><p>The imaginary coordinates of \\(D\\) range over \\((-1,1)\\), and for every \\(t\\in(-1,1)\\) there is a finite \\(V(t)&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\{v\\in\\mathbb R:v+it\\in D\\}=(-V(t),V(t)).\n\\]</p><p>Write\n\\[\nF(ir)=iT(r),\\qquad 0\\leq r&lt;1.\n\\]\nThen \\(T\\) increases strictly from \\(0\\) to \\(1\\), and\n\\[\nT'(r)=\\frac8{\\pi^2}\\frac{\\operatorname{arctanh}r}{r}\n\\quad(0&lt;r&lt;1),\n\\qquad\n\\lim_{r\\uparrow1}T'(r)=+\\infty.\n\\]</p><p>For fixed \\(t\\in(-1,1)\\), put \\(r_0=T^{-1}(|t|)\\). The positive horizontal slice is parametrized by\n\\[\nw(r,t)=re^{i\\theta(r,t)},\n\\qquad\nF(w(r,t))=v(r,t)+it,\n\\qquad r_0&lt;r&lt;1,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n-\\frac\\pi2&lt;\\theta(r,t)&lt;\\frac\\pi2,\n\\]\nand \\(v(r,t)\\) increases strictly from \\(0\\) to \\(V(t)\\). The inverse biholomorphism is denoted by \\(g=F^{-1}:D\\to\\mathbb D\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Lemma 2.1. Fix \\(|t|&lt;1\\), and let \\(T(r_0)=|t|\\). For every \\(r\\in(r_0,1)\\), the strictly increasing imaginary coordinate on the right semicircle ranges from \\(-T(r)\\) to \\(T(r)\\). There is consequently exactly one angle \\(\\theta(r,t)\\) there with imaginary coordinate \\(t\\). It depends smoothly on \\(r\\), since its angular derivative is \\(A&gt;0\\).</p><p>Write\n\\[\nwF'(w)=A+iB.\n\\]\nImplicit differentiation at fixed \\(t\\) gives\n\\[\n\\partial_r\\theta=-\\frac{B}{rA},\n\\qquad\n\\partial_r v=\\frac Ar-B\\partial_r\\theta\n=\\frac{A^2+B^2}{rA}.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial r}(r,t)\n=\n\\frac{|wF'(w)|^2}{rA(r,\\theta(r,t))}&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>As \\(r\\downarrow r_0&gt;0\\), the angle tends to the appropriate imaginary-axis endpoint of the semicircle, so \\(v(r,t)\\to0\\). If \\(r_0=0\\), the same limit follows from \\(F(0)=0\\). Boundedness of \\(F\\) now shows that \\(v(r,t)\\) maps \\((r_0,1)\\) continuously and strictly increasingly onto an interval \\((0,V(t))\\), where \\(0&lt;V(t)&lt;\\infty\\).</p><p>This parametrization proves injectivity on each positive horizontal slice. The reflection identity\n\\[\nF(-\\overline w)=-\\overline{F(w)}\n\\]\ngives the negative slices, and the strict monotonicity of \\(T\\) gives injectivity on the imaginary axis. The right half-disk, imaginary axis, and left half-disk have images with positive, zero, and negative real parts, respectively. Hence \\(F\\) is globally injective.</p><p>Every point of its image has imaginary coordinate in \\((-1,1)\\), and every such coordinate occurs on the imaginary axis. Therefore\n\\[\n\\{v\\in\\mathbb R:v+it\\in D\\}=(-V(t),V(t)).\n\\]\nSince \\(F'\\neq0\\), the local holomorphic inverses combine into a holomorphic inverse \\(g:D\\to\\mathbb D\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The series defining \\(F\\) is absolutely summable on the closed disk.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(F\\) and \\(\\mathbb D\\) from Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(w=re^{i\\theta}\\), with \\(0&lt;r&lt;1\\), define\n  \\[\n  A(r,\\theta)=\\operatorname{Re}(wF'(w)).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The series defining \\(F\\) is absolutely summable on the closed disk. The function is bounded, odd, and commutes with conjugation, and\n\\[\nwF'(w)=\\frac8{\\pi^2}\\arctan w,\n\\qquad\n\\arctan w=\\frac1{2i}\\log\\frac{1+iw}{1-iw},\n\\]\nwhere the logarithm is the branch on the right half-plane that is real on the positive real axis. The derivative \\(F'\\) has no zeros on \\(\\mathbb D\\).</p><p>Furthermore,\n\\[\nA(r,\\theta)\n=\n\\frac4{\\pi^2}\\arctan\\frac{2r\\cos\\theta}{1-r^2},\n\\]\nso\n\\[\n0&lt;A(r,\\theta)&lt;\\frac2\\pi\n\\quad\\text{when }-\\frac\\pi2&lt;\\theta&lt;\\frac\\pi2.\n\\]</p><p>There is a real function \\(T\\) satisfying \\(F(ir)=iT(r)\\), and it is strictly increasing, with\n\\[\nT(0)=0,\\qquad\nT'(r)=\\frac8{\\pi^2}\\frac{\\operatorname{arctanh}r}{r},\n\\qquad\n\\lim_{r\\uparrow1}T(r)=1,\n\\qquad\n\\lim_{r\\uparrow1}T'(r)=+\\infty.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The series defining \\(F\\) is absolutely summable on the closed disk. Hence \\(F\\) is bounded, odd, and commutes with conjugation. Termwise differentiation in the open disk gives\n\\[\nwF'(w)=\\frac8{\\pi^2}\\arctan w,\n\\qquad\n\\arctan w=\\frac1{2i}\\log\\frac{1+iw}{1-iw}.\n\\]\nThe indicated logarithm is defined because\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Re}\\frac{1+iw}{1-iw}\n=\n\\frac{1-|w|^2}{|1-iw|^2}&gt;0\n\\qquad(w\\in\\mathbb D).\n\\]\nThis also shows that \\(\\arctan w=0\\) only when \\(w=0\\). Thus \\(F'\\) has no zeros away from the origin, and the series gives\n\\[\nF'(0)=\\frac8{\\pi^2}\\neq 0.\n\\]</p><p>The imaginary part of \\((1+iw)/(1-iw)\\), divided by its real part, is\n\\[\n\\frac{2r\\cos\\theta}{1-r^2},\n\\]\nand its argument lies in \\((-\\pi/2,\\pi/2)\\). Consequently\n\\[\nA(r,\\theta)\n=\n\\frac4{\\pi^2}\\arctan\\frac{2r\\cos\\theta}{1-r^2}.\n\\]\nOn the right semicircle,\n\\[\n0&lt;A(r,\\theta)&lt;\\frac2\\pi.\n\\]</p><p>The identities\n\\[\n\\partial_r F(re^{i\\theta})=\\frac{wF'(w)}r,\n\\qquad\n\\partial_\\theta F(re^{i\\theta})=iwF'(w)\n\\]\nshow that \\(\\operatorname{Re}F(w)&gt;0\\) in the right half-disk: its radial derivative is positive, and its value tends to zero at the origin. They also show that \\(\\operatorname{Im}F(re^{i\\theta})\\) increases strictly with \\(\\theta\\) on each right semicircle.</p><p>Oddness and conjugation symmetry give \\(F(ir)=iT(r)\\) with \\(T\\) real. Differentiating on the imaginary axis gives\n\\[\nT'(r)=\\frac8{\\pi^2}\\frac{\\operatorname{arctanh}r}{r}.\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(T\\) is strictly increasing, \\(T(0)=0\\), and \\(T'(r)\\) diverges as \\(r\\uparrow1\\). Since \\(F(r)\\) is real, the angular derivative identity also gives\n\\[\nT(r)=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}A(r,\\theta)\\,d\\theta.\n\\]\nThe integrand is bounded by \\(2/\\pi\\), and it tends to \\(2/\\pi\\) at every \\(\\theta\\in[0,\\pi/2)\\) as \\(r\\uparrow1\\). Dominated convergence therefore gives \\(T(r)\\to1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition 3.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Each \\(J_k\\) is smooth on \\(D\\), is odd in \\(v\\), and satisfies \\( \\partial_vJ_k(v,t) = k^2|g(v+it)|^{2k-2}|g'(v+it)|^2\\geq0. \\) There is a sequence \\(\\epsilon_k\\geq0\\), depending only on \\(F\\), with \\(\\epsilon_k\\to0\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the biholomorphism \\(F:\\mathbb D\\to D\\), its inverse \\(g\\), and the function \\(T\\) of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\geq 2\\) be an integer.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(v+it\\in D\\), define the signed slice integral\n  \\[\n  J_k(v,t)\n  =\n  k^2\\int_0^v\n  |g(h+it)|^{2k-2}|g'(h+it)|^2\\,dh.\n  \\]\n  The segment of integration lies in \\(D\\) by Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Each \\(J_k\\) is smooth on \\(D\\), is odd in \\(v\\), and satisfies\n\\[\n\\partial_vJ_k(v,t)\n=\nk^2|g(v+it)|^{2k-2}|g'(v+it)|^2\\geq0.\n\\]\nThere is a sequence \\(\\epsilon_k\\geq0\\), depending only on \\(F\\), with \\(\\epsilon_k\\to0\\), such that, simultaneously for every \\(v+it\\in D\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{|J_k(v,t)|}{k}\n\\leq\n\\frac\\pi4|g(v+it)|^{2k}+\\epsilon_k.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 3.1 gives smoothness, oddness, and the derivative formula. It remains to obtain the uniform estimate.</p><p>Fix \\(r_*\\in(1/2,1)\\). For a positive horizontal slice ending at \\(v+it\\), put\n\\[\nR=|g(v+it)|,\\qquad r_0=T^{-1}(|t|).\n\\]\nThe part of the original horizontal integral for which \\(|g|\\leq r_*\\), after division by \\(k\\), is uniformly bounded by\n\\[\nC_*kr_*^{2k-2},\n\\qquad\nC_*=\n\\operatorname{diam}(D)\n\\max_{|w|\\leq r_*}|F'(w)|^{-2}&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nIndeed, the radius is strictly increasing along the positive slice, so this portion is its initial segment ending at radius \\(\\min(R,r_*)\\), when \\(r_0\\leq r_*\\), and is empty otherwise. Every point of this segment lies in \\(F(\\{|w|\\leq r_*\\})\\), and its horizontal length is at most \\(\\operatorname{diam}(D)\\). This includes the case \\(R\\leq r_*\\), when it is the whole integral.</p><p>If an outer portion remains, put\n\\[\nr_a=\\max(r_0,r_*),\n\\qquad\nL_*=\\inf_{r_*\\leq r&lt;1}T'(r)&gt;0.\n\\]\nSince \\(T(r_a)\\geq T(r_0)=|t|\\), for \\(r&gt;r_a\\),\n\\[\nT(r)-|t|\n\\geq T(r)-T(r_a)\n\\geq L_*(r-r_a).\n\\]\nLemmas 3.1 and 3.2 therefore bound the outer contribution by\n\\[\n\\frac\\pi4(R^{2k}-r_a^{2k})\n+\n\\frac{C_0}{\\sqrt{L_*}}\\,\nk\\int_{r_a}^1\nr^{2k-1}\\sqrt{\\frac{1-r}{r-r_a}}\\,dr,\n\\qquad\nC_0=\\frac{3\\sqrt2\\,\\pi^2}{8}.\n\\]\nThe extension of the error integral from \\(R\\) to \\(1\\) uses nonnegativity and removes any dependence on \\(R-r_a\\). Lemma 3.3 gives\n\\[\nk\\int_{r_a}^1\nr^{2k-1}\\sqrt{\\frac{1-r}{r-r_a}}\\,dr\n\\leq1+\\frac\\pi e.\n\\]</p><p>Combining these estimates, and using symmetry when \\(v&lt;0\\), gives, for every \\(v+it\\in D\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{|J_k(v,t)|}{k}\n-\\frac\\pi4|g(v+it)|^{2k}\n\\leq\nC_*kr_*^{2k-2}\n+\n\\frac{C_0(1+\\pi/e)}{\\sqrt{L_*}}.\n\\]\nWhen there is no outer portion, the first term alone bounds the excess, so the same inequality remains valid. It is also valid at \\(v=0\\).</p><p>Define explicitly\n\\[\n\\epsilon_k\n=\n\\max\\left\\{\n0,\\ \n\\sup_{v+it\\in D}\n\\left(\n\\frac{|J_k(v,t)|}{k}\n-\\frac\\pi4|g(v+it)|^{2k}\n\\right)\n\\right\\}.\n\\]\nThe preceding estimate makes this supremum finite for every \\(k\\). For fixed \\(r_*\\), the first error term tends to zero as \\(k\\to\\infty\\). Moreover,\n\\[\nL_*\n\\geq\n\\frac8{\\pi^2}\\operatorname{arctanh}r_*\n\\longrightarrow\\infty\n\\quad\\text{as }r_*\\uparrow1.\n\\]\nGiven \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), first choose \\(r_*\\) so that the second error term is less than \\(\\varepsilon/2\\). For this fixed \\(r_*\\), choose \\(N\\) such that\n\\[\nC_*kr_*^{2k-2}&lt;\\varepsilon/2\n\\quad\\text{for every }k\\geq N.\n\\]\nThen \\(\\epsilon_k&lt;\\varepsilon\\) simultaneously on all of \\(D\\).</p><p>In particular, the height \\(t\\), initial radius \\(r_0\\), and final radius \\(R\\) may all vary with \\(k\\), with no separation from either endpoint. Hence \\(\\epsilon_k\\to0\\), proving the proposition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The function \\(J_k\\) is smooth, odd in \\(v\\), and satisfies \\( \\partial_vJ_k(v,t) = k^2|g(v+it)|^{2k-2}|g'(v+it)|^2. \\) For \\(v>0\\), \\( \\frac{J_k(v,t)}k = k\\int_{r_0}^R \\frac{r^{2k-1}}{A(r,\\theta(r,t))}\\,dr. \\) The …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(w=re^{i\\theta}\\in\\mathbb D\\), write\n  \\[\n  A(r,\\theta)\n  =\n  \\operatorname{Re}(wF'(w))\n  =\n  \\frac4{\\pi^2}\\arctan\\frac{2r\\cos\\theta}{1-r^2}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(v&gt;0\\) and \\(v+it\\in D\\), put\n  \\[\n  R=|g(v+it)|,\\qquad r_0=T^{-1}(|t|),\n  \\]\n  and use the slice angle \\(\\theta(r,t)\\) from Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The function \\(J_k\\) is smooth, odd in \\(v\\), and satisfies\n\\[\n\\partial_vJ_k(v,t)\n=\nk^2|g(v+it)|^{2k-2}|g'(v+it)|^2.\n\\]\nFor \\(v&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{J_k(v,t)}k\n=\nk\\int_{r_0}^R\n\\frac{r^{2k-1}}{A(r,\\theta(r,t))}\\,dr.\n\\]\nThe integral at the lower endpoint is understood as an improper integral. The contribution obtained by replacing \\(1/A\\) with \\(\\pi/2\\) is\n\\[\n\\frac\\pi4(R^{2k}-r_0^{2k}).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For any fixed \\(v+it\\in D\\), the horizontal segment from \\(it\\) to \\(v+it\\) is a compact subset of the open set \\(D\\); the same is true for all sufficiently nearby endpoints and heights. The formula\n\\[\nJ_k(v,t)\n=\nk^2v\\int_0^1\n|g(av+it)|^{2k-2}|g'(av+it)|^2\\,da\n\\]\ntherefore proves smoothness by differentiation under the integral. The integrand is smooth even at the zero of \\(g\\), since\n\\[\n|g|^{2k-2}=(|g|^2)^{k-1}\n\\]\nand \\(k\\) is an integer. The derivative formula follows from the fundamental theorem of calculus.</p><p>The symmetry\n\\[\ng(-\\overline\\zeta)=-\\overline{g(\\zeta)}\n\\]\nmakes the density in the definition of \\(J_k\\) even in its real coordinate. Thus\n\\[\nJ_k(-v,t)=-J_k(v,t),\n\\]\nand it suffices to estimate \\(v&gt;0\\).</p><p>Along the positive horizontal slice, the radius increases from \\(r_0\\) to \\(R\\). Since\n\\[\ng'(F(w))=\\frac1{F'(w)}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial r}\n=\n\\frac{|wF'(w)|^2}{rA},\n\\]\nwe have\n\\[\n|g'|^2\\,dv=\\frac rA\\,dr.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n\\frac{J_k(v,t)}k\n=\nk\\int_{r_0}^R\n\\frac{r^{2k-1}}{A(r,\\theta(r,t))}\\,dr.\n\\]</p><p>At the lower endpoint, perform the change of variables first on a segment beginning at a positive real coordinate, and then let that coordinate decrease to zero. The original integrand is smooth on the full compact segment, so its integral is finite; the nonnegative transformed integrals converge to the same value. This argument also covers \\(t=0\\), when \\(r_0=0\\).</p><p>Since \\(0&lt;A&lt;2/\\pi\\), split \\(1/A\\) into \\(\\pi/2\\) and the nonnegative excess \\(1/A-\\pi/2\\). The constant part contributes\n\\[\nk\\frac\\pi2\\int_{r_0}^Rr^{2k-1}\\,dr\n=\n\\frac\\pi4(R^{2k}-r_0^{2k}).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\frac1{A(r,\\theta(r,t))} \\leq \\frac\\pi2+ C_0\\sqrt{\\frac{1-r}{T(r)-|t|}}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(F,T,A\\) and the slice angle \\(\\theta(r,t)\\) from Propositions 2 and 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(t\\in(-1,1)\\), put \\(r_0=T^{-1}(|t|)\\), and suppose\n  \\[\n  \\frac12\\leq r&lt;1,\\qquad r&gt;r_0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  C_0=\\frac{3\\sqrt2\\,\\pi^2}{8}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\frac1{A(r,\\theta(r,t))}\n\\leq\n\\frac\\pi2+\nC_0\\sqrt{\\frac{1-r}{T(r)-|t|}}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Set\n\\[\ns=\\frac\\pi2-|\\theta|\\in(0,\\pi/2],\n\\qquad\nX=\\frac{2r\\sin s}{1-r^2}.\n\\]\nFor every \\(X&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\frac1{\\arctan X}-\\frac2\\pi\\leq\\frac2X.\n\\]\nFor \\(X\\leq1\\), this follows from \\(\\arctan X\\geq X/2\\). For \\(X\\geq1\\), use\n\\[\n\\frac\\pi2-\\arctan X=\\arctan(1/X)\\leq1/X,\n\\qquad\n\\arctan X\\geq\\pi/4.\n\\]\nCombining this estimate with\n\\[\n\\sin s\\geq\\frac{2s}\\pi,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{1+r}{2r}\\leq\\frac32,\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\n\\frac1{A(r,\\theta)}\n\\leq\n\\frac\\pi2+\\frac{3\\pi^3}{8}\\frac{1-r}{s}.\n\\]</p><p>On the other hand, the vertical gap to the semicircle endpoint is\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta(r,\\theta)\n&amp;:=T(r)-|\\operatorname{Im}F(re^{i\\theta})|\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\int_0^s\n\\frac4{\\pi^2}\n\\arctan\\frac{2r\\sin\\xi}{1-r^2}\\,d\\xi\\\\\n&amp;\\leq\n\\frac{4r}{\\pi^2(1-r^2)}s^2\n\\leq\n\\frac2{\\pi^2}\\frac{s^2}{1-r}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nHere we used \\(\\arctan y\\leq y\\) and \\(\\sin\\xi\\leq\\xi\\). The last inequality supplies the lower bound\n\\[\ns\\geq\\frac\\pi{\\sqrt2}\\sqrt{\\Delta(r,\\theta)(1-r)}.\n\\]\nAlong the slice, \\(\\Delta(r,\\theta(r,t))=T(r)-|t|\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\frac1{A(r,\\theta(r,t))}\n\\leq\n\\frac\\pi2+\n\\frac{3\\sqrt2\\,\\pi^2}{8}\n\\sqrt{\\frac{1-r}{T(r)-|t|}}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The bounds \\( I(k,r_a)\\leq 1+\\frac{\\pi H}{2}e^{-H/2} \\leq1+\\frac\\pi e \\) and \\( I(k,r_a)\\leq\\frac{\\pi H}{2} \\) hold uniformly, including when \\(r_a\\) depends on \\(k\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\geq2\\) be an integer and \\(1/2&lt;r_a&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  I(k,r_a)\n  =\n  k\\int_{r_a}^1\n  r^{2k-1}\\sqrt{\\frac{1-r}{r-r_a}}\\,dr,\n  \\qquad\n  H=k(1-r_a).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The bounds\n\\[\nI(k,r_a)\\leq\n1+\\frac{\\pi H}{2}e^{-H/2}\n\\leq1+\\frac\\pi e\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nI(k,r_a)\\leq\\frac{\\pi H}{2}\n\\]\nhold uniformly, including when \\(r_a\\) depends on \\(k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The substitution \\(y=k(1-r)\\) gives\n\\[\nI(k,r_a)\n=\n\\int_0^H\n(1-y/k)^{2k-1}\\sqrt{\\frac y{H-y}}\\,dy\n\\leq\n\\int_0^H\ne^{-y}\\sqrt{\\frac y{H-y}}\\,dy,\n\\]\nsince \\(\\log r\\leq-(1-r)\\) and \\(2k-1\\geq k\\).</p><p>On \\([0,H/2]\\), the square-root factor is at most one. On \\([H/2,H]\\), bound the exponential by \\(e^{-H/2}\\) and use\n\\[\n\\int_0^H\\sqrt{\\frac y{H-y}}\\,dy=\\frac{\\pi H}{2}.\n\\]\nIt follows that\n\\[\nI(k,r_a)\n\\leq\n1+\\frac{\\pi H}{2}e^{-H/2}\n\\leq1+\\frac\\pi e.\n\\]\nThe same integral without its exponential factor also gives\n\\[\nI(k,r_a)\\leq\\frac{\\pi H}{2}.\n\\]\nThese estimates hold for every \\(H&gt;0\\), covering both \\(H\\downarrow0\\) and \\(H\\to\\infty\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Proposition 4.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(0<c<4\\), there is a smooth symplectic embedding \\( B^{2n}(c)\\longrightarrow \\operatorname{int}K\\times\\operatorname{int}K^\\circ. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\geq2\\) be an integer.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(b_1,\\ldots,b_m\\in\\mathbb R^n\\) span \\(\\mathbb R^n\\), and define\n  \\[\n  K=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^n:|b_j\\cdot x|\\leq1,\\ 1\\leq j\\leq m\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K^\\circ\\) be its polar.</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(F:\\mathbb D\\to D\\) and \\(g=F^{-1}\\) from Proposition 2, and \\(J_k,\\epsilon_k\\) from Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Extend each real functional complex-linearly:\n  \\[\n  \\ell_j(z)=b_j\\cdot z.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\Omega=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C^n:\\ell_j(z)\\in D\n  \\text{ for every }j\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For each fixed integer \\(k\\geq2\\), use the notation\n  \\[\n  f_j(z)=g(\\ell_j(z))^k,\\qquad\n  \\tau(z)=\\sum_{j=1}^m|f_j(z)|^2,\n  \\qquad\n  V=\\{z\\in\\Omega:\\tau(z)&lt;1\\},\n  \\qquad\n  \\omega=dd^c(|z|^2+\\tau).\n  \\]\n  Here \\(d^c\\) has the convention of Proposition 1; the objects \\(f_j,\\tau,V,\\omega\\) depend on \\(k\\), whereas \\(K,\\Omega\\), and the list \\(b_1,\\ldots,b_m\\) are fixed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(0&lt;c&lt;4\\), there is a smooth symplectic embedding\n\\[\nB^{2n}(c)\\longrightarrow\n\\operatorname{int}K\\times\\operatorname{int}K^\\circ.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For every fixed \\(k\\geq2\\), Lemma 4.1 verifies the hypotheses of Proposition 1 for the tuple\n\\[\nf_j(z)=g(\\ell_j(z))^k.\n\\]\nThus \\((V,\\omega)\\) contains symplectic images of all standard balls of capacity less than \\(\\pi k\\). Lemma 4.2 realizes this form by the symplectic embedding \\(\\Phi_k\\).</p><p>Fix \\(0&lt;c&lt;4\\). Choose \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) with\n\\[\n(1+\\eta)c&lt;4,\n\\]\nand then fix \\(k\\) large enough for the inclusion in Lemma 4.3. With\n\\[\nS=(1+\\eta)\\frac{\\pi k}{4},\n\\]\nwe have \\(Sc&lt;\\pi k\\). Proposition 1 therefore gives a symplectic embedding\n\\[\nE:B^{2n}(Sc)\\longrightarrow(V,\\omega).\n\\]</p><p>Let\n\\[\nD_{\\sqrt S}(z)=\\sqrt S\\,z,\n\\qquad\nL_S(q,p)=(q,p/S).\n\\]\nThe composition\n\\[\nL_S\\circ\\Phi_k\\circ E\\circ D_{\\sqrt S}\n:\nB^{2n}(c)\\longrightarrow\n\\operatorname{int}K\\times\\operatorname{int}K^\\circ\n\\]\nis a smooth embedding. Since \\(D_{\\sqrt S}\\) maps \\(B^{2n}(c)\\) onto \\(B^{2n}(Sc)\\) and\n\\[\nD_{\\sqrt S}^*\\omega_0=S\\omega_0,\n\\qquad\nL_S^*\\omega_0=S^{-1}\\omega_0,\n\\]\nits pullback of \\(\\omega_0\\) is exactly \\(\\omega_0\\). This proves the proposition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The set \\(K\\) is a compact convex body containing \\(0\\) in its interior, and \\(\\Omega\\) is a bounded open neighborhood of \\(0\\), independent of \\(k\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting and construction notation of Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix an integer \\(k\\geq2\\), and put \\(f=(f_1,\\ldots,f_m)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The set \\(K\\) is a compact convex body containing \\(0\\) in its interior, and \\(\\Omega\\) is a bounded open neighborhood of \\(0\\), independent of \\(k\\).</p><p>The holomorphic tuple \\(f:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb C^m\\) satisfies\n\\[\nf^{-1}(0)=\\{0\\},\n\\qquad\n|f(z)|=O(|z|^k)\\quad\\text{as }z\\to0.\n\\]\nFor every \\(0&lt;s&lt;1\\), the sublevel\n\\[\n\\{z\\in\\Omega:\\tau(z)\\leq s\\}\n\\]\nis a compact subset of \\(\\Omega\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The spanning condition makes the linear map\n\\[\nz\\longmapsto(\\ell_1(z),\\ldots,\\ell_m(z))\n\\]\ninjective, so there is a constant \\(C_b\\) such that\n\\[\n|z|\\leq C_b\\max_j|\\ell_j(z)|.\n\\]\nSince \\(D\\) is bounded, \\(\\Omega\\) is bounded as well; neither set depends on \\(k\\). The same linear bound makes \\(K\\) compact, and a sufficiently small Euclidean ball lies in all its defining strips. In particular, \\(0\\in\\operatorname{int}K\\). The set \\(\\Omega\\) is an open neighborhood of the origin because \\(D\\) is open and contains \\(0\\).</p><p>The tuple \\(f\\) is holomorphic on \\(\\Omega\\). Since \\(g\\) has its only zero at the origin, its common zero set is\n\\[\n\\{z\\in\\Omega:\\ell_j(z)=0\\text{ for every }j\\}=\\{0\\}.\n\\]\nHolomorphicity at the origin gives \\(|f(z)|=O(|z|^k)\\).</p><p>Fix \\(0&lt;s&lt;1\\). On \\(\\{\\tau\\leq s\\}\\), for every \\(j\\),\n\\[\n\\ell_j(z)\\in\nF\\bigl(\\{w\\in\\mathbb C:|w|\\leq s^{1/(2k)}\\}\\bigr),\n\\]\na compact subset of \\(D\\). The linear bound makes this sublevel bounded. Moreover, the limit of any convergent sequence in it still has all its images under the functionals \\(\\ell_j\\) in that compact subset of \\(D\\), hence belongs to \\(\\Omega\\) and satisfies \\(\\tau\\leq s\\) by continuity. Thus the sublevel is compact in \\(\\Omega\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The map \\(\\Phi_k\\) is a smooth, globally injective symplectic embedding:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting of Proposition 4, with \\(k\\geq2\\) fixed.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(z=u+ix\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Equip the target \\(\\mathbb R_q^n\\times\\mathbb R_p^n\\) with\n  \\[\n  \\omega_0=\\sum_{\\ell=1}^n dq_\\ell\\wedge dp_\\ell.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\Phi_k:V\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R_q^n\\times\\mathbb R_p^n,\n  \\qquad\n  q=x,\\qquad\n  p=-u-\\sum_{j=1}^mJ_k(b_j\\cdot u,b_j\\cdot x)b_j.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The map \\(\\Phi_k\\) is a smooth, globally injective symplectic embedding:\n\\[\n\\Phi_k^*\\omega_0=\\omega.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The signed integrals \\(J_k\\) are smooth on \\(D\\) by Proposition 3, so \\(\\Phi_k\\) is smooth.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nv_j=b_j\\cdot u,\\qquad t_j=b_j\\cdot x.\n\\]\nPulling back the target form gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi_k^*\\omega_0\n&amp;=\n\\sum_{\\ell=1}^n dx_\\ell\\wedge(-du_\\ell)\n-\\sum_{j=1}^m dt_j\\wedge dJ_k(v_j,t_j)\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\sum_{\\ell=1}^n du_\\ell\\wedge dx_\\ell\n+\\sum_{j=1}^m\n\\partial_vJ_k(v_j,t_j)\\,dv_j\\wedge dt_j\\\\\n&amp;=\ndd^c(|z|^2+\\tau)=\\omega.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nIndeed, the terms containing \\(\\partial_tJ_k\\) vanish because \\(dt_j\\wedge dt_j=0\\), while\n\\[\n\\partial_vJ_k(v,t)\n=\nk^2|g(v+it)|^{2k-2}|g'(v+it)|^2.\n\\]\nThis is precisely the coefficient of\n\\[\ndd^c|g(v+it)^k|^2\n\\]\nin front of \\(dv\\wedge dt\\), with the fixed convention for \\(d^c\\).</p><p>To prove global injectivity, equality of the \\(q\\) coordinates first fixes \\(x\\). For two possible real coordinates \\(u_1,u_2\\) at that \\(x\\), write\n\\[\n\\Delta u=u_2-u_1,\\qquad\nv_{ji}=b_j\\cdot u_i,\\qquad\nt_j=b_j\\cdot x,\n\\]\nand denote their momenta by \\(p_1,p_2\\). Both \\(v_{j1}\\) and \\(v_{j2}\\) belong to the same horizontal interval of \\(D\\) at height \\(t_j\\). Since \\(\\partial_vJ_k\\geq0\\) on this entire interval,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(p_2-p_1)\\cdot\\Delta u\n&amp;=\n-|\\Delta u|^2\\\\\n&amp;\\quad-\n\\sum_{j=1}^m\n\\bigl(J_k(v_{j2},t_j)-J_k(v_{j1},t_j)\\bigr)\n(v_{j2}-v_{j1})\\\\\n&amp;\\leq-|\\Delta u|^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus equal momenta force \\(u_1=u_2\\). This comparison applies to any two points of the fiber, even if that fiber of \\(V\\) is disconnected.</p><p>The pullback identity and nondegeneracy of \\(\\omega\\) show that \\(\\Phi_k\\) is a local diffeomorphism. It is therefore open, and injectivity gives a smooth inverse onto its image. Consequently \\(\\Phi_k\\) is a symplectic embedding.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a finite constant \\(C_K\\), independent of \\(k\\), such that, for every \\(z=u+ix\\in V\\), writing \\(\\Phi_k(z)=(q,p)\\), \\( q=x\\in\\operatorname{int}K \\) and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting of Proposition 4 and the map \\(\\Phi_k\\) of Lemma 4.2.</p><p class=\"li\">Define the support function\n  \\[\n  h_K(p)=\\max_{y\\in K}\\langle p,y\\rangle.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a finite constant \\(C_K\\), independent of \\(k\\), such that, for every \\(z=u+ix\\in V\\), writing \\(\\Phi_k(z)=(q,p)\\),\n\\[\nq=x\\in\\operatorname{int}K\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\frac{h_K(p)}k\n\\leq\n\\frac{C_K}k+\\frac\\pi4\\tau(z)+m\\epsilon_k.\n\\]\nConsequently, for any \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) and all sufficiently large \\(k\\),\n\\[\n\\Phi_k(V)\\subset\n\\operatorname{int}K\\times S\\operatorname{int}K^\\circ,\n\\qquad\nS=(1+\\eta)\\frac{\\pi k}{4}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>All imaginary parts of points of \\(D\\) lie in \\((-1,1)\\), so\n\\[\n|b_j\\cdot x|&lt;1\n\\quad\\text{for every }j.\n\\]\nThe finite system of strict inequalities implies \\(q=x\\in\\operatorname{int}K\\).</p><p>The support function satisfies\n\\[\nh_K(\\pm b_j)\\leq1,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{int}K^\\circ=\\{p:h_K(p)&lt;1\\}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(\\Omega\\) is bounded, there is a finite constant \\(C_K\\), independent of \\(k\\), such that\n\\[\nh_K(-u)\\leq C_K\\quad\\text{on }\\Omega.\n\\]\nSubadditivity and positive homogeneity of \\(h_K\\), followed by Proposition 3, give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{h_K(p)}k\n&amp;\\leq\n\\frac{C_K}k+\n\\sum_{j=1}^m\\frac{|J_k(v_j,t_j)|}k\\\\\n&amp;\\leq\n\\frac{C_K}k+\n\\frac\\pi4\\sum_{j=1}^m|g(\\ell_j(z))|^{2k}\n+m\\epsilon_k\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\frac{C_K}k+\\frac\\pi4\\tau(z)+m\\epsilon_k.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nHere \\(\\epsilon_k\\geq0\\) tends to zero uniformly in the planar argument. The number \\(m\\) and the constant \\(C_K\\) are fixed before \\(k\\) varies.</p><p>Given \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), for all sufficiently large \\(k\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{C_K}k+m\\epsilon_k&lt;\\frac{\\eta\\pi}{4}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\tau&lt;1\\) on \\(V\\), the preceding estimate gives\n\\[\nh_K(p)&lt;(1+\\eta)\\frac{\\pi k}{4}.\n\\]\nThis proves the stated inclusion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Lemma 5.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are finitely many vectors \\(b_1,\\ldots,b_m\\in\\mathbb R^n\\) spanning \\(\\mathbb R^n\\) such that \\( K'=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^n:|\\langle b_j,x\\rangle|\\leq1,\\ 1\\leq j\\leq m\\} \\) satisfies …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\) be an origin-symmetric convex body.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K^\\circ\\) denote its polar.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are finitely many vectors \\(b_1,\\ldots,b_m\\in\\mathbb R^n\\) spanning \\(\\mathbb R^n\\) such that\n\\[\nK'=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^n:|\\langle b_j,x\\rangle|\\leq1,\\ 1\\leq j\\leq m\\}\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\nK\\subset K'\\subset(1+\\alpha)K,\n\\qquad\n(K')^\\circ\\subset K^\\circ.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>First, \\(0\\in\\operatorname{int}K\\). Indeed, if the Euclidean ball \\(B(y,r)\\) is contained in \\(K\\), then so is \\(B(-y,r)\\), and their midpoints contain \\(B(0,r)\\). Since \\(K\\) is also bounded, there are \\(r,R&gt;0\\) with\n\\[\nB(0,r)\\subset K\\subset B(0,R).\n\\]\nThe definition of the polar gives\n\\[\n\\overline{B(0,R^{-1})}\\subset K^\\circ\n\\subset\\overline{B(0,r^{-1})}.\n\\]\nThus \\(K^\\circ\\) is a compact, origin-symmetric convex body with nonempty interior.</p><p>We use the bipolar identity \\(K^{\\circ\\circ}=K\\). If \\(x\\notin K\\), separation gives a nonzero vector \\(v\\) such that\n\\[\n\\langle v,x\\rangle&gt;h_K(v),\n\\qquad\nh_K(v)=\\max_{y\\in K}\\langle v,y\\rangle&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe normalized vector \\(v/h_K(v)\\) belongs to \\(K^\\circ\\) and pairs with \\(x\\) to a value greater than \\(1\\), so \\(x\\notin K^{\\circ\\circ}\\). The reverse inclusion follows directly from the definition. The same argument applies to every convex body containing the origin in its interior.</p><p>Put\n\\[\n\\lambda=1+\\alpha,\n\\qquad\n\\rho=\\min_{|v|=1}h_{K^\\circ}(v)&gt;0.\n\\]\nChoose \\(d&gt;0\\) with\n\\[\nd\\leq\\frac{\\alpha\\rho}{1+\\alpha},\n\\]\nand choose a finite \\(d\\)-net \\(\\{b_1,\\ldots,b_m\\}\\) in the compact set \\(K^\\circ\\), with all net points in \\(K^\\circ\\). Let\n\\[\nP=\\operatorname{conv}\\{\\pm b_1,\\ldots,\\pm b_m\\}.\n\\]\nSymmetry of \\(K^\\circ\\) gives \\(P\\subset K^\\circ\\). For every unit vector \\(v\\), a maximizer of \\(\\langle v,p\\rangle\\) over \\(p\\in K^\\circ\\) lies within distance \\(d\\) of a net point. Consequently\n\\[\nh_P(v)\\geq h_{K^\\circ}(v)-d\n\\geq\\lambda^{-1}h_{K^\\circ}(v).\n\\]\nThe characterization of inclusion by support functions, which follows from convex separation, gives\n\\[\n\\lambda^{-1}K^\\circ\\subset P\\subset K^\\circ.\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(P\\) has interior, so its generating vectors span \\(\\mathbb R^n\\).</p><p>Taking polars and using the bipolar identity yields\n\\[\nK\\subset P^\\circ\\subset\\lambda K,\n\\qquad\n(P^\\circ)^\\circ=P\\subset K^\\circ.\n\\]\nFinally, the definition of \\(P\\) shows that \\(P^\\circ\\) is exactly the finite strip body \\(K'\\) in the statement.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "the upper-bound part of the proof of Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a symplectic embedding \\( U_K\\hookrightarrow B^2(4)\\times\\mathbb R^{2n-2}. \\) Consequently, by Gromov’s nonsqueezing theorem, \\( c_G(U_K)\\leq4. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\geq2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\) be an origin-symmetric convex body, with polar \\(K^\\circ\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  U_K=\\operatorname{int}K\\times\\operatorname{int}K^\\circ,\n  \\qquad\n  \\omega_0=\\sum_{j=1}^n dq_j\\wedge dp_j.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  B^2(4)=\\{(X,Y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:\\pi(X^2+Y^2)&lt;4\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a symplectic embedding\n\\[\nU_K\\hookrightarrow B^2(4)\\times\\mathbb R^{2n-2}.\n\\]\nConsequently, by Gromov’s nonsqueezing theorem,\n\\[\nc_G(U_K)\\leq4.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the supporting-slab construction of Artstein-Avidan, Karasev, and Ostrover, cited in the paper in [1, Remark 4.2]. Choose\n\\[\nq_0\\in K,\\qquad p_0\\in K^\\circ,\n\\qquad\n\\langle q_0,p_0\\rangle=1.\n\\]\nSuch a pair is obtained by maximizing any nonzero linear functional on \\(K\\) and normalizing its maximum to \\(1\\).</p><p>Complete \\(q_0\\) to a basis of \\(\\mathbb R^n\\) by vectors in \\(\\ker p_0\\), and let \\(A\\) be the matrix of this basis. The linear change\n\\[\nQ=A^{-1}q,\\qquad P=A^Tp\n\\]\nis symplectic and has first coordinates\n\\[\nQ_1=\\langle p_0,q\\rangle,\n\\qquad\nP_1=\\langle q_0,p\\rangle.\n\\]\nBoth functionals are nonzero, so symmetry, polarity, and the interior condition give\n\\[\n|Q_1|&lt;1,\\qquad |P_1|&lt;1\n\\quad\\text{on }U_K.\n\\]\nThus this change embeds \\(U_K\\) into\n\\[\n(-1,1)^2\\times\\mathbb R^{2n-2},\n\\]\nwith the square in its first conjugate coordinate pair.</p><p>This open square is area-preservingly diffeomorphic to the disk of area \\(4\\). Put\n\\[\nR=\\frac2{\\sqrt\\pi},\n\\qquad\nh(X)=\\sqrt{R^2-X^2}\\quad(-R&lt;X&lt;R),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nH(X)=-1+\\int_{-R}^Xh(s)\\,ds.\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\n\\int_{-R}^Rh(s)\\,ds=2,\n\\qquad H'=h&gt;0,\n\\]\nthe function \\(H\\) is a smooth diffeomorphism from \\((-R,R)\\) onto \\((-1,1)\\). The map\n\\[\n(u,v)\\longmapsto\n\\bigl(X=H^{-1}(u),\\ Y=v\\,h(H^{-1}(u))\\bigr)\n\\]\nhas image \\(B^2(4)\\) and Jacobian\n\\[\nX'(u)h(X)=1.\n\\]\nApplying it to the first conjugate pair gives a symplectic embedding\n\\[\nU_K\\hookrightarrow B^2(4)\\times\\mathbb R^{2n-2}.\n\\]\nGromov’s nonsqueezing theorem, cited in the paper in [5], bounds the capacity of every ball in \\(U_K\\) by \\(4\\).</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 2, "cite"], [3, 13, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [5, 3, "cite"], [6, 3, "cite"], [7, 9, "cite"], [7, 10, "cite"], [7, 11, "cite"], [7, 13, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [9, 13, "cite"], [9, 15, "cite"], [9, 16, "cite"], [10, 9, "cite"], [11, 9, "cite"], [12, 9, "cite"], [13, 0, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [15, 13, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [16, 13, "cite"], [17, 0, "cite"], [18, 0, "cite"]]});
